\cf8 \cb9 \uc0\u10095  \cf10 Add four secondary post-transplant outcomes to the harmonized GVHD metadata.\cf2     \cb1 \
\cb9                                                                                   \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10 Follow the "Harmonization Variable Additions" section of CLAUDE.md for all\cf2       \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10 standing conventions.\cf2                                                            \cb1 \
\cb9                                                                                   \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10 SHAPE: four binary covariates\cf2                                                    \cb1 \
\cb9                                                                                   \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10 DEFINITION: Secondary post-transplant complications, recorded as simple 1/0\cf2      \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10 event flags. No time-to-event columns and no severity columns for any of these \cf2  \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10 \'97\cf2                                                                                \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10 where a source records a grade, collapse it to presence/absence. Most are\cf2        \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10 single-study; heavy NA is expected and fine.\cf2                                     \cb1 \
\cb9                                                                                   \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10 COLUMNS (all 1/0, all added to binary_outcome_cols):\cf2                             \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10   myelosuppression          myelosuppression during the transplant course\cf2        \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10   mucositis                 mucositis (oral/GI) during the transplant course\cf2     \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10   bloodstream_infection     documented bacteraemia or fungaemia post-transplant\cf2  \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10   bronchiolitis_obliterans  bronchiolitis obliterans syndrome\cf2                    \cb1 \
\cb9                                                                                   \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10 PLACEMENT: at the end of target_cols, after relapse_day_rel_transplant, in the\cf2   \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10 order listed above. These are secondary outcomes and sit behind the primary\cf2      \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10 GVHD, death and relapse blocks.\cf2                                                  \cb1 \
\cb9                                                                                   \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10 PER-STUDY CONSTRUCTION:\cf2                                                          \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10 - Artacho:  Myelosuppression 0/1 -> myelosuppression (74 yes / 98 no).\cf2           \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10             Mucositis Yes/No -> mucositis (15 yes / 157 no).\cf2                     \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10             Other new columns -> NA.\cf2                                             \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10 - DAmico:   Mucositis_grade -> mucositis. Grades I, II and III -> 1. Both "/"\cf2    \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10             and NA -> 0, i.e. treat an ungraded patient as having no mucositis.\cf2  \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10             Expect 94 yes / 10 no.\cf2                                               \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10             BSI -> bloodstream_infection. The column is free text naming\cf2         \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10             organisms and days ("S.aureus (+7)"); any non-missing value -> 1,\cf2    \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10             NA -> 0. NA genuinely means no BSI here, confirmed by the \cf2           \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10 manuscript\cf2                                                                       \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10             (all events were in the PN group; the EN group had zero). Expect\cf2     \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10             39 yes / 65 no.\cf2                                                      \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10             Other new columns -> NA.\cf2                                             \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10 - Fujimoto: not recorded -> NA for all four.\cf2                                     \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10 - Ingham:   not recorded -> NA for all four.\cf2                                     \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10 - Liu2017:  bloodstream_infection derived by READING the two free-text \cf2          \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10 infection\cf2                                                                        \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10             fields. See the LIU BSI CLASSIFICATION section below.\cf2                \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10             Other new columns -> NA.\cf2                                             \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10 - Vallet:   bos -> bronchiolitis_obliterans. "yes" -> 1; missing -> 0, NOT NA.\cf2   \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10             The dictionary states explicitly that NA/missing means no BOS, and\cf2   \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10             manuscript Table 1 reports BOS counts of 0, 0, 1, 1 by enterotype,\cf2   \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10             confirming exactly 2 cases. Expect 2 yes / 116 no.\cf2                   \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10             Other new columns -> NA.\cf2                                             \cb1 \
\cb9                                                                                   \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10 LIU BSI CLASSIFICATION:\cf2                                                          \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10 Liu records infections as unstructured free text across two columns,\cf2             \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10 infection_duringafter_transplant (45 distinct non-blank values among the 57\cf2      \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10 recipients) and infection_details (27 distinct non-blank values). Do not write \cf2  \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10 a\cf2                                                                                \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10 regex. Instead:\cf2                                                                  \cb1 \
\cb9                                                                                   \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10   1. Extract the complete list of distinct values from each column, restricted\cf2   \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10      to recipient rows.\cf2                                                          \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10   2. Read every one of those ~72 strings and decide, per string, whether it\cf2      \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10      documents a bloodstream infection.\cf2                                          \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10      - Any documented bacteraemia counts as a BSI. If the text names \cf2            \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10 bacteraemia\cf2                                                                      \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10        (however spelled, and including constructions like "CNS bacteremia",\cf2      \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10        "E coli bacteremia 12/2013", "bacteremia: pseudomonas, E coli, VRE"),\cf2     \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10        classify it 1.\cf2                                                            \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10      - Fungaemia and candidaemia count as BSI.\cf2                                   \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10      - Non-bloodstream infections do not count: UTI, pneumonia, colitis,\cf2         \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10        cellulitis, sinusitis, abscess, oral candida, C difficile, and similar.\cf2   \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10      - Viral viremia (CMV, EBV, BK, adenovirus) does not count. BSI here means\cf2   \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10        bacteraemia or fungaemia.\cf2                                                 \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10      - Organism names appearing without any site or bloodstream context are\cf2      \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10        ambiguous. Use your judgement, and list every call you were unsure about\cf2  \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10        in the decision log so I can review them.\cf2                                 \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10   3. Hard-code the resulting classification into harmonize_gvhd_metadata.R as \cf2   \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10 an\cf2                                                                               \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10      explicit literal lookup: two named character vectors (or a small tibble)\cf2    \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10      mapping each distinct source string to 1 or 0. R cannot read free text at\cf2   \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10      runtime, so the reading happens once, now, and the code must contain the\cf2    \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10      finished crosswalk rather than a heuristic. Every distinct value must \cf2      \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10 appear\cf2                                                                           \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10      in the lookup \'97 including the blank/NA value, which maps to 0.\cf2              \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10   4. A recipient is bloodstream_infection = 1 if EITHER column's value maps to \cf2  \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10 1.\cf2                                                                               \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10      Donor rows -> NA.\cf2                                                           \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10   5. Add a QA table that reproduces the full crosswalk (source string, column,\cf2   \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10      classification, n samples) so the derivation stays auditable, and a test\cf2    \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10      asserting that every distinct source value in the real data is present in\cf2   \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10      the lookup \'97 so the script fails loudly rather than silently returning NA\cf2   \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10      if the source file ever changes.\cf2                                            \cb1 \
\cb9                                                                                   \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10 For reference, a strict regex on bacteremia|bacteraemia|fungemia|candidemia|\cf2     \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10 septic shock|sepsis gives 13 of 57 recipients. Treat that as a sanity-check\cf2      \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10 ballpark, not a target \'97 if your reading lands far from it, say why.\cf2             \cb1 \
\cb9                                                                                   \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10 NOTES:\cf2                                                                           \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10 - Artacho's Myelosuppression belongs here, as its own outcome. If a \cf2             \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10 conditioning\cf2                                                                     \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10   block already exists in the file, confirm Myelosuppression was NOT used to\cf2     \cb1 \
\cb9   \cf10   populate conditioning_intensity \'97 it is a toxicity of conditioning, not a\cf2      \cb1 \
